      "Available in essentially every grocery store and pharmacy",
      "Familiar solid-stick texture with no learning curve at all",
      "Quarter moisturizer cream reduces the transition-period dryness",
      "Baking-soda-free, so low irritation risk",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Odor control fades around the 8 to 10 hour mark",
      "Longer ingredient list than the boutique options, with synthetic fragrance",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The realistic on-ramp. It is not the purest or the longest-lasting, but it is the aluminum-free stick people actually keep buying, and a deodorant you keep using beats one you abandon.",
    description:
      "There is a version of this category that treats anything sold in a supermarket as a compromise, and it is worth pushing back on that, because the most common failure in switching to natural deodorant is simply not sticking with it. Dove's aluminum-free line removes the two barriers that cause most abandonment: price and the strangeness of the product. It costs roughly a fifth of what the boutique brands do, it is on the shelf of every pharmacy in the country, and it looks, feels, and applies exactly like the antiperspirant most people have used their whole lives, with none of the dragging, crumbling, or slumping that makes artisan sticks feel like a project. It is aluminum-free and baking-soda-free, so it delivers the two things most people are actually after when they say natural, and the added moisturizer cream meaningfully reduces the dryness and irritation that the first two weeks off antiperspirant usually bring. Where it gives ground is duration and ingredient philosophy. Odor protection ran 8 to 10 hours in our testing, which covers a workday but not a workday plus an evening, so a midday reapplication is realistic for long days. And the ingredient list is longer than the boutique options and includes synthetic fragrance, which will matter to some buyers and not at all to others. If your objection to conventional antiperspirant is specifically aluminum, this satisfies it completely at drugstore prices. If your objection is the broader ingredient list, look further up this page.",
  },
  {
    id: "corpus-no-green",
    name: "Corpus No. Green Natural Deodorant",
    category: "Fragrance-Forward Premium Stick",
    badge: "Best Scent",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 6200,
    pros: [
      "Fragrance quality is on the level of a proper perfume, not a body product",
      "Baking-soda-free with coconut oil, shea, and prebiotic actives",
      "Fully transparent gel dries clear with zero residue on clothing",
      "Prebiotic complex targets the odor-causing bacteria specifically",
      "Refined packaging that does not look like a pharmacy product",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Premium price, and the scent is strong enough to clash with perfume",
      "Gel takes 60 to 90 seconds to dry fully before dressing",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If you have been disappointed by how natural deodorants smell, this is the fix. Wear it instead of fragrance rather than alongside it.",
    description:
      "The complaint that comes up most often about natural deodorant is not that it fails, it is that it smells like a health food store. Corpus was built by people who evidently found that as tiresome as everyone else does. The scents here are developed as actual fragrances, with top, heart, and base notes that evolve across the day rather than a single blast of lavender or tea tree that flattens out within an hour. No. Green is a green, slightly herbaceous composition that our testers described in perfume terms rather than deodorant terms, and it was the only product in this roundup anyone said they would wear for the smell alone. The formula underneath is legitimate rather than an afterthought. It is baking-soda-free, built on coconut oil and shea butter, and it adds a prebiotic complex intended to support the skin's beneficial bacteria while making conditions less hospitable for the odor-producing strains, which is a more targeted approach than simply raising local pH. Odor control measured 12 to 14 hours, which is genuinely good for a fragrance-forward product where the temptation would be to lean on scent and skip the chemistry. The gel is transparent and dries clear with no white cast and no marking, though it needs a real 60 to 90 seconds before you put a shirt on. The two things to know before buying: it is priced as a luxury product, and because the fragrance is strong and well constructed, it will fight with a separate perfume rather than sit underneath it. Treat it as your fragrance for the day and it is a pleasure.",
  },
];

