    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the difference between deodorant and antiperspirant?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Deodorant addresses odor by neutralizing or absorbing the compounds bacteria produce when they break down sweat. Antiperspirant addresses wetness by using aluminum salts to form temporary plugs in the sweat ducts. Every natural deodorant is a deodorant, not an antiperspirant, because aluminum salts are the only ingredient class the FDA recognizes for reducing sweat. You will still sweat on a natural deodorant.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long does the natural deodorant transition period last?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most people need two to four weeks. Coming off antiperspirant, sweat volume returns to normal within a few days and the underarm bacterial community rebalances over the following weeks, which is why odor is often worse before it gets better. Odor typically peaks around days 4 to 10 and settles by week three or four. Judge a natural deodorant after a month, not after a week.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is aluminum in antiperspirant actually dangerous?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The evidence does not support a link between antiperspirant aluminum and breast cancer or Alzheimer's disease. The National Cancer Institute, the American Cancer Society, and the Alzheimer's Association all state there is no established causal connection, and large studies have not found one. Choosing aluminum-free is a legitimate personal preference, and there are good reasons for it, but you should not be frightened into it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why does baking soda deodorant cause a rash?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Baking soda has a pH around 9, while healthy underarm skin sits near 5.5. That alkaline mismatch disrupts the skin's acid mantle, and for roughly one in five to one in three people it produces redness, itching, burning, or a bumpy rash, typically appearing after one to three weeks rather than immediately. The fix is switching to a magnesium hydroxide or mandelic acid formula, not pushing through it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do natural deodorants stain clothing?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They can, but differently than antiperspirant. The yellow stains on white shirts come from aluminum reacting with sweat proteins, so switching away from antiperspirant usually reduces them. Natural formulas instead risk oil marks from coconut oil or shea butter bases and grey transfer from activated charcoal. Apply two or three swipes rather than six, and let the product dry fully before dressing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you use natural deodorant right after shaving?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Wait at least 20 to 30 minutes, and longer if you have any nicks. Shaving removes a thin layer of skin along with hair, so applying baking soda or an AHA formula immediately afterward stings and increases irritation risk. Shaving at night and applying deodorant in the morning is the simplest way to avoid the problem entirely.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does natural deodorant work for heavy sweaters?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It controls the odor but not the wetness, because no natural deodorant reduces sweat volume. If wetness is your primary concern, a natural deodorant alone will disappoint you. Options include pairing it with an absorbent powder, using antiperspirant only on days you need it, or, for genuine hyperhidrosis, seeing a dermatologist about clinical-strength or prescription treatment.",
          },
