        <!-- SECTION 1: Methodology -->
        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How We Tested
          </h2>
          <p>
            Deodorant is close to impossible to evaluate honestly on yourself. You acclimate to your own scent within minutes, which is precisely why the person wearing a failing deodorant is the last to know. Any test that relies on self-reported odor is measuring confidence, not performance. So we built the test around other people's noses.
          </p>
          <p>
            Six testers — four who had used conventional antiperspirant continuously for over a decade, and two who had already switched and had a documented history of baking soda irritation — wore 16 natural deodorants across 90 days. Each product got a minimum of seven consecutive days on a single tester, which matters because natural deodorant performance changes as underarm bacteria adjust; a one-day trial tells you almost nothing. Testers rotated products so that every formula was worn by at least three different people, since underarm chemistry varies enormously between individuals and a deodorant that fails on one person can be excellent on another.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What We Measured</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Odor control, scored by someone else.</strong> At the 4, 8, 12, and 16-hour marks, a panel member who had not been told which product was in use rated underarm odor on a 5-point scale at conversational distance. Testers also self-rated, and the gap between the two scores turned out to be one of the more useful things we recorded — self-ratings were consistently about a point more generous. The durations quoted in this guide are the panel's numbers, not the wearers'.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Performance under load.</strong> Every product was worn through at least two hard training sessions and, because our test ran through July, several days above 90 degrees Fahrenheit. Office-day performance and gym-day performance diverge sharply, so we report both.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Skin reaction.</strong> Testers logged redness, itching, burning, and any rash daily, along with when it appeared. Timing matters here: baking soda reactions characteristically show up in week two or three rather than on day one, which is exactly why so many people fail to connect the rash to the product.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Clothing transfer.</strong> Each product was worn with a white cotton shirt and a black cotton shirt on separate days, and both were photographed before laundering. This is where charcoal formulas and heavy coconut oil bases separate themselves from the rest.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The practical stuff.</strong> Application feel, dry-down time, whether the stick crumbled or dragged, whether it survived a gym bag in summer, and how far one tube went. These sound trivial next to odor control and they are the reasons people actually abandon a product.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Disqualified a Product</h3>
          <p>
            Eight of the 16 did not make this list. Three failed on duration, dropping below acceptable panel scores before the 8-hour mark on an ordinary day, which is not enough to get through a workday. Two were cut for irritation severe enough that a tester stopped mid-week, in both cases high-baking-soda formulas without a gentler alternative in the range. One was cut for a stick that crumbled into unusable chunks within two weeks. Two more were simply beaten: a product on this list did the same job better at the same price, and there is no value in a roundup that recommends everything.
          </p>

