                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mandelic acid</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Blocks the odor-forming enzyme</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-green-700">24-72 hrs</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-yellow-600">Low, stings on shaved skin</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Charcoal / absorbents</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adsorb odor and surface moisture</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Support role</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-green-700">Very low</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            One ingredient worth flagging for a specific group: essential oils. They provide the fragrance in most natural deodorants and several, notably tea tree and lavender, have real antibacterial activity. They are also among the more common causes of contact allergy in cosmetics. If you have sensitive or allergy-prone skin, a genuinely fragrance-free formula removes an entire category of risk, and it is worth starting there before blaming the active.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Product Reviews -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The 8 Best Natural Deodorants in 2026
          </h2>
          <p>
            Each of these was worn for at least a week at a time by at least three testers, scored blind by a panel at four checkpoints a day, and put through gym sessions and a July heat wave. Here is what earned a place.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-x-auto border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left min-w-[640px]">
