        <!-- SECTION 6: Transition -->
        <div id="transition" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. The Transition Period, Week by Week
          </h2>
          <p>
            This is the section we would put first if search engines let us, because more switches fail here than anywhere else. Coming off antiperspirant, most people go through two to four weeks in which they smell worse than they did before. If you do not know that is coming, you conclude the product failed and you go back — usually somewhere around day six, which is almost exactly the worst possible day to judge.
          </p>
          <p>
            A note on the word detox, which you will see everywhere. Your armpits are not detoxing. They are not organs of elimination and there are no toxins leaving through them. What is actually happening is more prosaic and more useful to understand: the aluminum plugs in your sweat ducts dissolve and clear over several days, so sweat volume returns to its natural level and feels like a dramatic increase by comparison. Meanwhile the underarm microbiome, which has spent years in a low-moisture environment, rebalances toward its normal population, and the transitional mix of species produces more odor than either the before or after state. That readjustment is real, it is temporary, and it has nothing to do with toxins.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <table class="w-full text-left">
              <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                <tr>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Timeline</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What Happens</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What to Do</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Days 1-3</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Noticeably more sweat as duct plugs clear. Odor still mild.</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Start on a weekend. Wear dark or loose tops.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Days 4-10</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The worst stretch. Odor peaks as bacteria rebalance.</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Reapply midday. Wash with a gentle cleanser, not antibacterial soap.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Days 11-21</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Steady improvement. Sweat normalizes, odor drops off.</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Hold the course. Watch for irritation, which peaks here too.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Weeks 4+</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">New baseline. This is the product's real performance.</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-500">Now judge it. Switch formulas if it is still falling short.</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Make the Transition Easier</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Start on a Friday.</strong> The worst days fall between day four and day ten, and having a weekend inside that window rather than a run of client meetings makes a real difference to whether you stick with it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Do not use antibacterial soap.</strong> The instinct is to scrub harder, and it is exactly wrong. Antibacterial cleansers wipe out the beneficial species along with the odor-producing ones, and the ones that recolonize fastest are typically the ones you do not want. Use a mild, pH-balanced cleanser and wash normally.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consider a clay mask in the first week.</strong> A thin bentonite clay mask on the underarms, left five to ten minutes once or twice, seems to speed the shift for some people and is harmless either way. Do not do this daily, and do not do it if your skin is already irritated.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reapply without guilt.</strong> Midday reapplication during weeks one and two is normal and not a sign the product is failing. Carry the tube.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Wear natural fibers.</strong> Cotton, linen, and wool release odor compounds in the wash. Polyester and other synthetics trap them in the fiber, which is why a gym shirt can smell the moment it warms up even after laundering. During the transition especially, what you wear affects how you smell as much as what you apply.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Give it a month before judging.</strong> If after four weeks a formula is still failing you before midday, it is the wrong formula — switch actives rather than brands, moving from magnesium to baking soda for more strength, or to mandelic acid for more duration.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Irritation -->
        <div id="irritation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Rashes, Irritation, and How to Fix Them
          </h2>
          <p>
            The most damaging piece of advice circulating about natural deodorant is that a rash is part of the detox and you should push through it. You should not. Detox, properly understood, involves more sweat and more odor. It does not involve inflamed, itching, burning skin. If your underarms are visibly irritated, something in the product is irritating them, and continuing will make it worse.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Identifying What Is Actually Wrong</h3>
          <p>
            <strong>Baking soda irritation</strong> presents as diffuse redness with small bumps, a burning or stinging sensation especially after showering, and skin that feels tight or looks slightly shiny. The signature is timing: it appears in week two or three, not immediately, because the barrier disruption is cumulative. It is also usually symmetrical across both underarms and worse on the side you shave more aggressively.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Essential oil allergy</strong> looks different. It tends toward well-defined patches, sometimes with small blisters, and it itches more than it burns. It can appear at any point, including after months of uneventful use, because contact allergy develops with repeated exposure rather than on first contact.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Folliculitis</strong> is distinct from both: individual inflamed bumps centered on hair follicles, sometimes with a visible white point. It usually traces to shaving rather than to the deodorant, though occlusive product on freshly shaved skin makes it more likely.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Stop Using It If</p>
            <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">
              The skin is broken, weeping, or cracked; the irritation spreads beyond where you applied the product; you develop swelling or hives; or the rash has not improved within a week of stopping. Persistent underarm rashes that do not resolve after discontinuing the product should be looked at by a dermatologist, since inverse psoriasis, contact dermatitis, and fungal infections all present similarly in skin folds and are treated differently.
            </p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Recovery Protocol</h3>
          <p>
            Stop the product entirely — not reduce it, stop it. Give the skin barrier five to seven days with nothing applied but a plain, fragrance-free moisturizer; a simple ceramide cream works well. Do not shave during this window, because shaving irritated skin extends the recovery substantially. Avoid antibacterial or heavily fragranced body wash. If the itching is significant, over-the-counter hydrocortisone used sparingly for a few days is reasonable, though it is not for long-term use in skin folds.
          </p>
          <p>
            Once the skin is fully calm, restart with a different active class rather than a different brand of the same thing. If baking soda was the culprit, go to magnesium hydroxide or mandelic acid. Patch test first: apply a small amount to the inner forearm daily for three days and watch. It is not a perfect predictor, since underarm skin is thinner and occluded in a way forearm skin is not, but it catches the strongest reactions cheaply.
          </p>
          <p>
            One more preventive habit worth adopting permanently: separate shaving and deodorant application in time. Shave at night, apply deodorant in the morning. That single change removes most of the sting-on-application complaints in this category, and it matters more with AHA formulas than with anything else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Application -->
        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Application, Storage, and Keeping It Off Your Clothes
          </h2>
          <p>
            Natural deodorant is less forgiving of technique than antiperspirant, and a surprising share of complaints about greasiness, pilling, and marked shirts come down to how it is being used rather than what is in it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Much, and When</h3>
          <p>
            Two to three swipes per underarm for a stick. A pea-sized amount per side for a cream. That is genuinely all, and over-application is the single most common error we saw. A thicker layer does not extend protection — the active can only work at the skin surface, and everything beyond that sits on top waiting to transfer to your shirt. If you are not getting enough duration, the answer is a different formula or a midday touch-up, never a heavier coat.
          </p>
          <p>
            Apply to clean, completely dry skin. Damp skin after a shower dilutes the product and prevents it adhering properly, and residual moisture is exactly what encourages the bacteria you are trying to discourage. Give it a full 60 to 90 seconds to dry down before dressing, which is longer than most people wait and is the difference between a clean shirt and a marked one, particularly with gels and serums.
          </p>
          <p>
            The counterintuitive tip that works: apply at night as well as in the morning. Because these actives work on the skin surface rather than by blocking ducts, giving them uninterrupted overnight contact on clean, dry, non-sweating skin establishes a much better starting position for the following day. Several testers found that a nightly application plus a morning one comfortably closed the gap on formulas that had been falling short by early evening.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Storage and Shelf Life</h3>
          <p>
            Most natural formulas are built on coconut oil, shea butter, or tallow, and all of those soften well below body temperature — coconut oil melts around 76 degrees Fahrenheit. A stick left in a car, a gym locker, or a sunny bathroom in July will slump or separate, and once it has melted and resolidified the texture rarely recovers. Store it somewhere cool and dry, and if you carry one in a bag through summer, prefer the firmer magnesium-based sticks over coconut-heavy ones.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those same fats also go rancid. Most natural deodorants carry a period-after-opening symbol, typically 12 months, and because they use minimal preservatives that figure is realistic rather than conservative. A rancid deodorant smells faintly sour or crayon-like, and it should be replaced rather than used up. This is a good argument against buying the value four-pack of a product you have not tested.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stains, Marks, and Laundry</h3>
          <p>
            The good news first: the stiff yellow staining that ruins white shirts comes from aluminum reacting with sweat proteins, so switching away from antiperspirant usually reduces it over time. Natural deodorants create two different problems instead. Oil-based formulas can leave translucent grease marks, most visible on dark fabric, and charcoal formulas can transfer visible grey to light fabric. Both are caused by over-application and by dressing before the product has dried.
          </p>
          <p>
            For existing marks, treat oil transfer before washing: a small amount of dish soap worked into the fabric with a soft brush, left 15 minutes, then laundered warm, lifts most of it. Never put a marked garment through the dryer before the stain is gone, since heat sets oil permanently. For built-up underarm odor in synthetic athletic fabrics, which is a fiber problem rather than a deodorant problem, a 30-minute soak in a solution of white vinegar and water before a normal wash works better than any amount of detergent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 9: Who should buy what -->
        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which One Is Right for You
          </h2>
          <p>
            Rankings are a starting point. What you should actually buy depends on which of these describes you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You have never used natural deodorant before</h3>
          <p>
            Start with Native Classic. It has the longest duration of any stick we tested, so the transition period is less discouraging, and it is available everywhere, which removes the reordering friction that quietly ends a lot of switches. If your skin objects in week two or three, move to Native's own sensitive version or to Each &amp; Every, and do not conclude the category has failed you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Baking soda has given you a rash before</h3>
          <p>
            Skip every baking soda formula, including the ones with a small amount buried in the ingredient list. Go straight to Each &amp; Every for the simplest formula and lowest risk, or to Schmidt's Magnesium and Charcoal for better duration at a lower price. Do not try to build tolerance. It does not work that way.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You want to think about it as little as possible</h3>
          <p>
            Lume. The mandelic acid mechanism delivers duration nothing else here approaches, and a single morning application genuinely lasted our testers a full day and often longer. You will need to make peace with a cream rather than a stick and with a brief scent on application, and in exchange you stop carrying a tube.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You train hard or work outdoors</h3>
          <p>
            Understand first that nothing in this category reduces sweat — you will be wet, and the goal is not smelling. Lume holds up best through repeat sessions. Schmidt's charcoal formula is the best stick option under load. Whatever you choose, keep it in your bag rather than your car in summer, wear natural fibers where you can, and expect to reapply after a session rather than before.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Your underarm skin is dark, rough, or bumpy from years of shaving</h3>
          <p>
            Kosas Chemistry AHA is the only pick here that treats that as the problem to solve. Expect meaningful texture change by week four and visible tone improvement by week eight, alongside decent odor control. It costs more than anything else on this list, so it makes sense specifically when the skin, not just the smell, is what you want to fix.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You mainly object to aluminum and do not want to overthink it</h3>
          <p>
            Dove Zero Aluminum. It is aluminum-free and baking-soda-free, it costs a fraction of the boutique brands, it is stocked everywhere, and it feels exactly like the antiperspirant you are used to. Duration is the shortest here at 8 to 10 hours, so long days need a touch-up. A deodorant you keep buying beats a purer one you abandon in March.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The ingredient list is the whole point for you</h3>
          <p>
            Primally Pure, at five ingredients with nothing synthetic, is as short as this category gets while still working well. It does contain baking soda, so it is off the table if you are sensitive. If you want both a short list and no baking soda, Each &amp; Every's six-ingredient formula is the better fit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">You have tried natural deodorant and hated how it smelled</h3>
          <p>
            Corpus. The fragrances are built like actual perfumes rather than a blast of tea tree, and it was the only product in this test anyone said they would wear for the scent alone. Wear it instead of your usual fragrance rather than underneath it, and give the gel a full minute and a half to dry.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-3">If You Only Remember One Thing</p>
            <p class="text-gray-800">
              Pick by active ingredient, not by brand. Baking soda for maximum strength if your skin tolerates it, magnesium hydroxide if it does not, mandelic acid if duration is what you care about most. Everything else — the scent, the packaging, the story on the label — is preference. The active is the thing that determines whether this works on you.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 10: Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #1</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Quitting on Day Six</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Odor peaks between days four and ten as the underarm microbiome rebalances, which is precisely when most people give up and decide natural deodorant does not work. Judge a product at four weeks, not at one. Starting on a Friday puts the worst days on a weekend.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #2</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Pushing Through a Rash</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">A rash is not detox. Detox means more sweat and more odor, not inflamed skin. Baking soda irritation shows up in week two or three, which is why it gets misread as part of the process. Stop, let the barrier recover, and switch to a magnesium or AHA formula.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #3</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Expecting It to Stop the Sweat</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">No natural deodorant reduces sweat volume, because aluminum salts are the only ingredient class that does. You will sweat and you will not smell. If visible wetness is your actual problem, this category cannot solve it, and shopping around within it will not change that.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #4</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Applying Far Too Much</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Two or three swipes, not six. The active only works at the skin surface, so a thicker layer adds nothing but transfer onto your shirt. Nearly every complaint about greasiness, pilling, and marked clothing traces back to over-application and to dressing before it dried.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #5</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Applying Straight After Shaving</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Shaving takes a thin layer of skin with the hair, so baking soda and AHA formulas sting and irritate on freshly shaved underarms. Wait 20 to 30 minutes at minimum, and ideally shave at night and apply in the morning.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #6</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Scrubbing With Antibacterial Soap</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Harsh antibacterial washes strip the beneficial underarm bacteria along with the odor-producing ones, and the species that recolonize fastest are often the wrong ones. Use a mild, pH-balanced cleanser, and do not wash more aggressively than usual during the transition.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #7</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Buying the Multi-Pack First</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">Individual response in this category varies more than almost any other product we test, and natural formulas go rancid within about a year of opening. Buy one, wear it for a month, and only then stock up on the one that actually worked on you.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-2 border-red-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-red-700 mb-2">Mistake #8</p>
              <h4 class="font-bold text-lg text-gray-900 mb-2">Trusting the Word Natural</h4>
              <p class="text-red-900 text-sm">The term has no regulatory definition in US cosmetics, so any product can use it. Read the ingredient panel and look for certifications with real standards behind them — USDA Organic, EWG Verified, Leaping Bunny — rather than the adjective on the front of the tube.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between deodorant and antiperspirant?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Deodorant addresses odor by neutralizing or absorbing the compounds bacteria produce when they break down sweat. Antiperspirant addresses wetness, using aluminum salts to form temporary plugs in the sweat ducts. Every natural deodorant is a deodorant, not an antiperspirant, because aluminum salts are the only ingredient class the FDA recognizes for reducing sweat. You will still sweat on a natural deodorant — you just should not smell.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does the natural deodorant transition period last?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Most people need two to four weeks. Coming off antiperspirant, sweat volume returns to normal within a few days as the duct plugs clear, and the underarm bacterial community rebalances over the following weeks — which is why odor often gets worse before it gets better. It typically peaks around days 4 to 10 and settles by week three or four. Judge a natural deodorant at a month, not at a week.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Is aluminum in antiperspirant actually dangerous?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">The evidence does not support a link between antiperspirant aluminum and breast cancer or Alzheimer's disease. The National Cancer Institute, the American Cancer Society, and the Alzheimer's Association all state there is no established causal connection, and larger studies have not found one. Choosing aluminum-free is a legitimate personal preference — less shirt staining, a shorter ingredient list — but you should not be frightened into it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does baking soda deodorant cause a rash?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Baking soda has a pH around 9, while healthy underarm skin sits near 5.5. That alkaline mismatch disrupts the skin's acid mantle, and for roughly one in five to one in three people it produces redness, itching, burning, or a bumpy rash. The tell is timing: it appears after one to three weeks rather than immediately, which is why it gets mistaken for detox. The fix is switching to magnesium hydroxide or mandelic acid, not pushing through.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Do natural deodorants stain clothing?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">They can, but differently. The stiff yellow stains on white shirts come from aluminum reacting with sweat proteins, so switching away from antiperspirant usually reduces them. Natural formulas instead risk translucent oil marks from coconut oil or shea bases and grey transfer from activated charcoal. Apply two or three swipes rather than six and let the product dry fully before dressing, and both largely disappear.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use natural deodorant right after shaving?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Wait at least 20 to 30 minutes, and longer if you have any nicks. Shaving removes a thin layer of skin along with the hair, so applying a baking soda or AHA formula immediately afterward stings and raises the irritation risk considerably. The simplest fix is to shave at night and apply deodorant in the morning, which removes the problem entirely.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does natural deodorant work for heavy sweaters?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">It controls the odor but not the wetness, because no natural deodorant reduces sweat volume. If wetness is your primary concern, a natural deodorant alone will disappoint you. Reasonable options are pairing it with an absorbent powder, using antiperspirant only on the days you need it, or — for genuine hyperhidrosis — seeing a dermatologist about clinical-strength or prescription treatment.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">How much natural deodorant should you apply?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Two to three swipes per underarm for a stick, or a pea-sized amount for a cream. Over-application is the most common user error in this category and causes most complaints about greasiness, pilling, and marked clothing without improving odor control at all. If you are not getting enough protection, the answer is a different formula or a midday reapplication, not a thicker layer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Which natural deodorant lasts the longest?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">In our testing, mandelic acid formulas lasted longest by a wide margin — 24 hours or more from one application — because they block the enzymatic reaction that creates odor rather than fighting bacteria continuously. Among traditional sticks, baking soda formulas ran 13 to 16 hours and magnesium hydroxide formulas 10 to 14. Individual variation is large, so treat these as a ranking rather than a guarantee.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h4 class="font-bold text-xl text-gray-900 mb-2">Does natural deodorant expire?</h4>
              <p class="text-gray-700">Yes. Formulas built on coconut oil, shea butter, or tallow contain fats that eventually go rancid, typically within 12 to 18 months of opening, and most carry a period-after-opening symbol on the packaging. A rancid deodorant smells faintly sour or crayon-like. Because natural formulas use minimal preservatives, store them somewhere cool and dry rather than in a steamy bathroom — and skip the multi-pack until you know a product works for you.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

